# 14. CHAINS — TYPED COMPUTATIONAL COMPOSITION

## 14.1 Definition

A Chain represents a typed computational graph connecting operations through declared dependencies.

Chains combine the clarity of ordered pipelines with the expressiveness of dependency graphs.

Each stage has a defined:

- Identity.
- Input.
- Output.
- Type.
- Dependency set.
- Effect summary.
- Ownership behavior.
- Failure contract.
- Execution constraint.

## 14.2 Linear chains

Linear Chains execute dependent operations in declared order.

```pcn
chain AccountReview
    stage identify = identify(account)
    stage validate = check(account)
    stage evaluate = evaluate(validate)
    stage reply = respond(evaluate)
```

## 14.3 Nonlinear chains

Nonlinear Chains represent branching and converging dependencies.

```pcn
chain Approval
    stage identity = verify_identity(request)
    stage limits = verify_limits(request)

    stage decision after identity, limits
        if identity and limits
            give approved

        give denied
```

Independent stages retain their dependency and effect rules.

## 14.4 Parallel execution

A dependency graph does not automatically authorize concurrency.

Parallel stages require established independence, compatible effects, legal ownership and appropriate synchronization.

Scheduling follows an explicit runtime execution policy.

## 14.5 Chain optimization

The compiler performs:

- Stage specialization.
- Pure-stage fusion.
- Redundant-stage elimination.
- Dependency simplification.
- Closure scalarization.
- Direct-call lowering.
- Valid traversal fusion.
- Resource-aware scheduling.

Every optimization preserves observable behavior.

## 14.6 Cross-Capsule chains

Protected Chains use typed authorization, preparation, execution, response and failure protocols.

External side effects remain subject to their actual reversibility and durability guarantees.

