# 20. CONCRETE, BORROWED AND DYNAMIC CLOSURES

Paracine supports three distinct closure classes.

| Closure class | Representation |
|---|---|
| Concrete owned | Statically typed environment with known callable |
| Borrowing | Statically checked capture lifetimes |
| Dynamic owned | Explicit type-erased callable storage and dispatch |

## 20.1 Concrete owned closures

Concrete owned closures remain movable and returnable under ordinary ownership rules.

They do not require boxing merely because they are returned from a routine.

## 20.2 Borrowing closures

Borrowing closures cannot escape the lifetime of their captured references.

Allocating a box does not repair an invalid borrowed lifetime.

## 20.3 Dynamic closures

Dynamic closures provide explicit heterogeneous callable representation.

```pcn
routine make_multiplier(factor: int)
    with alloc
    gives result<own dyn_closure<(int), int>, AllocationError>

    let operation =
        move routine(value: int) gives int => value * factor

    give try box_closure(operation)
```

Dynamic closure storage carries allocation, ownership, disposal and dispatch obligations.

## 20.4 Foreign callbacks

Capturing closures cannot be silently converted into environment-free foreign routine pointers.

Foreign callbacks use declared callback/userdata protocols, compatible ABI adapters, valid environment lifetimes and explicit teardown.

## 20.5 Optimization

The compiler scalarizes or specializes dynamic representations only when their identity, allocation, dispatch, lifetime and externally observable behavior remain equivalent.

