# 28. FORMAL POLR DECISION AND COST MODELS

## 28.1 Hard feasibility

Let \(P\) be the candidate route set.

Let \(F\subseteq P\) contain routes satisfying all mandatory obligations.

Feasibility requires semantic validity, ownership legality, authority, isolation, contracts, ABI compatibility and governing resource constraints.

An invalid path is eliminated before cost optimization.

## 28.2 Cost vector

For each feasible path:

\[
C(p)=
(
C_{\mathrm{complexity}},
C_{\mathrm{change}},
C_{\mathrm{runtime}},
C_{\mathrm{memory}},
C_{\mathrm{verification}},
C_{\mathrm{maintenance}},
C_{\mathrm{risk}}
)
\]

Each metric retains units, evidence and estimation provenance.

## 28.3 Weighted objective

\[
C_{\mathrm{POLR}}(p)
=
\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_i\widehat{C_i(p)}
\]

where weights represent explicit engineering priorities.

The selected route is:

\[
p^*=
\operatorname*{arg\,min}_{p\in F}
C_{\mathrm{POLR}}(p)
\]

The optimality statement applies to the evaluated feasible candidate set.

Bounded search does not imply exhaustive discovery of every theoretically possible solution.

## 28.4 Pareto evaluation

For conflicting objectives, POLR retains nondominated feasible alternatives.

The declared engineering profile determines the final recommendation.

Stable tie-breaking preserves deterministic results.

## 28.5 Operating profiles

| Profile | Priority |
|---|---|
| `balanced` | Overall engineering and execution efficiency |
| `performance` | Runtime speed |
| `memory` | Storage footprint and allocation |
| `security` | Enforceable isolation and reduced authority |
| `minimal-change` | Narrow source and dependency impact |
| `maintainability` | Clear architecture and controlled complexity |
| `compile-throughput` | Faster valid semantic and native compilation |
| `resource-constrained` | Strict CPU and memory budgets |
| `verification` | Straightforward proof and conformance obligations |

No profile overrides mandatory language correctness.

