# **PART I — LANGUAGE AND EXECUTION RESOLUTIONS**

## **NLR-01 — Complete syntax and lexical authority**

### **1.1 Keyword classification**

Paracine 2027 SHALL recognize three distinct word classes.

**Reserved keywords** cannot be used as ordinary identifiers. They include the retained Edition-2026 vocabulary, together with:

```
capsule family chain extension protocol
checkpoint commit rollback revert recover
ask respond self
```

The Edition-2026 reserved set includes its declaration, control-flow, ownership, operator and effect vocabulary, together with `public`, `private`, `export`, `to`, `until`, `return_` and `yield_`.

**Contextual keywords** have special meaning only in designated grammatical positions:

```
isolate inherit member stage after capture
contract invariant shared
allow deny avoid limit zone
init override virtual abstract final sealed
request response error
using within
```

For example, `stage` is an ordinary identifier outside a Chain declaration.

**Ordinary identifiers** include library operation names such as `fill`, `cut`, `trim`, `scale`, `taper`, `train`, `identify` and `availability`. They are not reserved merely because they appear in the advanced feature register.

This preserves namespace flexibility.

### **1.2 Lexical grammar**

The compiler SHALL first produce a stream of normalized lexical tokens.

```
IDENT
INTEGER
FLOATING
STRING
CHARACTER
NEWLINE
INDENT
DEDENT
EOF
```

The lexical specification SHALL require:

- Unicode 15.1 XID identifiers, normalized to NFC.
- ASCII digits for source-level numeric syntax.
- No implicit multiplication or juxtaposition.
- Four ASCII spaces per indentation level.
- Indentation increases of exactly one level at a time.
- Maximal-munch operator tokenization.
- Layout suppression inside matched grouping delimiters.
- No implicit statement termination inside expressions.
- Original byte offsets retained independently of normalized text.
- Comments treated as trivia, without changing syntactic meaning.

Nested generic closers written `> >` are separate tokens. The adjacent spelling `>>` remains the shift operator, preserving parser-independent lexical classification.

### **1.3 Proposed declaration and statement grammar**

The following EBNF-style grammar fixes the structural syntax. `NL` represents a logical newline, while `INDENT` and `DEDENT` are produced by the lexical layout engine.

```

Source          = [ Module ], { Import | Declaration | NL }, EOF ;

Module          = "module", QualifiedName, NL ;
Import          = [ "public" ], "import", QualifiedName,
                  [ "as", Identifier ], NL ;

Declaration     = Attributes, [ Visibility ],
                  ( RoutineDecl | RecordDecl | VariantDecl
                  | CapabilityDecl | CapsuleDecl | FamilyDecl
                  | ChainDecl | ExtensionDecl | ProtocolDecl
                  | ContractDecl | AliasDecl | ForeignDecl
                  | ConstantDecl | StaticDecl ) ;

Visibility      = "private" | "public" | "export" ;

Attributes      = { Attribute, NL } ;
Attribute       = "@", Identifier, [ "(", AttributeArgs, ")" ] ;

RoutineDecl     = MethodModifiers, "routine", Identifier,
                  [ GenericParams ], "(", Parameters, ")",
                  [ EffectClause ], [ "gives", Type ],
                  NL, Suite ;

MethodModifiers = { "virtual" | "override" | "final"
                  | "abstract" } ;

EffectClause    = "with", EffectName, { ",", EffectName } ;
EffectName      = "alloc" | "io" | "thread"
                  | "trap" | "unsafe" | "pure" ;

Parameters      = [ Parameter, { ",", Parameter } ] ;
Parameter       = [ "own" | "var" | "ref" | "raw" ],
                  Identifier, ":", Type
                  | [ "own" | "var" ], "self" ;

RecordDecl      = "record", Identifier, [ GenericParams ],
                  NL, INDENT, { Field | NL }, DEDENT ;

VariantDecl     = "variant", Identifier, [ GenericParams ],
                  NL, INDENT, { VariantCase | NL }, DEDENT ;

CapsuleDecl     = "capsule", Identifier, [ GenericParams ],
                  [ "inherit", TypeName ],
                  NL, INDENT, { CapsuleMember | NL }, DEDENT ;

CapsuleMember   = IsolationDecl | PolicyDecl | Field
                  | Constructor | Declaration
                  | InvariantDecl ;

IsolationDecl   = "isolate", ("logical" | "memory" | "deep"), NL ;
InvariantDecl   = "contract", "invariant", Expression, NL ;

Constructor     = [ Visibility ], "init", "(",
                  Parameters, ")", [ "gives", Type ],
                  NL, Suite ;

FamilyDecl      = "family", Identifier, [ GenericParams ],
                  NL, INDENT, { FamilyMember | NL }, DEDENT ;

FamilyMember    = "member", TypeName, NL
                  | "contract", "shared", Identifier, NL
                  | "sealed", NL ;

ChainDecl       = "chain", Identifier, [ GenericParams ],
                  "(", Parameters, ")",
                  [ "gives", Type ],
                  NL, INDENT, { StageDecl | NL }, DEDENT ;

StageDecl       = "stage", Identifier,
                  [ "after", Identifier,
                    { ",", Identifier } ],
                  ( "=", Expression, NL | NL, Suite ) ;

ProtocolDecl    = "protocol", Identifier,
                  NL, INDENT,
                  RequestDecl, ResponseDecl, ErrorDecl,
                  DEDENT ;

RequestDecl     = "request", NL, INDENT, { Field }, DEDENT ;
ResponseDecl    = "response", NL, INDENT, { Field }, DEDENT ;
ErrorDecl       = "error", Type, NL ;

ExtensionDecl   = "extension", TypeName,
                  NL, INDENT, { Declaration }, DEDENT ;

ForeignDecl     = "foreign", "abi", StringLiteral,
                  NL, INDENT, { ForeignRoutine }, DEDENT ;

Suite           = INDENT, { Statement | NL }, DEDENT ;

Statement       = Binding | Assignment | ExpressionStmt
                  | IfStmt | ChooseStmt | WhileStmt
                  | ForStmt | DirectedLoop | PostTestLoop
                  | LoopStmt | CheckpointStmt
                  | RevertStmt | RollbackStmt | CommitStmt
                  | ReturnStmt | FailureStmt
                  | BreakStmt | ContinueStmt | DeferStmt
                  | RespondStmt ;

Binding         = ("let" | "var" | "const"), Identifier,
                  [ ":", Type ], "=", Expression, NL ;

Assignment      = Place, AssignOp, Expression, NL ;

IfStmt          = "if", Expression, NL, Suite,
                  [ "else", NL, Suite ] ;

WhileStmt       = "while", Expression, NL, Suite ;

ForStmt         = "for", Pattern, "in", Expression,
                  [ "while", Expression ], NL, Suite ;

DirectedLoop    = "from", Identifier, "=", Expression,
                  "to", Expression, NL, Suite ;

PostTestLoop    = "continue", "until", Expression,
                  NL, Suite ;

LoopStmt        = "loop", NL, Suite ;

CheckpointStmt  = "checkpoint", "@", Identifier,
                  NL, Suite,
                  [ "recover", Identifier, NL, Suite ] ;

RevertStmt      = "revert", "@", Identifier, NL ;
RollbackStmt    = "rollback", "@", Identifier,
                  [ "fail", Expression ], NL ;
CommitStmt      = "commit", "@", Identifier, NL ;

ReturnStmt      = "give", [ Expression ], NL ;
FailureStmt     = "fail", Expression, NL ;
RespondStmt     = "respond", Expression, NL ;

BreakStmt       = "break", [ Identifier ], NL ;
ContinueStmt    = "continue", [ Identifier ], NL ;
DeferStmt       = "defer", NL, Suite ;

```

Additional declarations such as `alias`, `static`, `const`, capability signatures, generic constraints, patterns, fields and policy clauses use ordinary typed productions. They MUST have their own closed grammar rather than being parsed as unrestricted token sequences.

### **1.4 Expression precedence**

The following table is normative, from highest to lowest binding strength.

| Precedence | Construct                                        | Associativity  |
| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------ | -------------- |
| 15         | Calls, member access, indexing, qualified access | Left           |
| 14         | Unary `-`, `+`, `!`, `not`, `~`, `move`, `try`   | Right          |
| 13         | Multiplication, division, remainder              | Left           |
| 12         | Addition, subtraction                            | Left           |
| 11         | Shifts `<<`, `>>`                                | Left           |
| 10         | Bitwise AND `&`                                  | Left           |
| 9          | Bitwise XOR `^`                                  | Left           |
| 8          | Bitwise OR `\\|`                                 | Left           |
| 7          | Ranges `..`, `..=`                               | Nonassociative |
| 6          | Comparisons `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, `is`, `in`     | Nonassociative |
| 5          | Equality `==`, `!=`                              | Nonassociative |
| 4          | Boolean `and`, `&&`                              | Left           |
| 3          | Boolean `or`, `\\|\\|`                           | Left           |
| 2          | Pipeline `\\|>`                                  | Left           |
| 1          | Explicit conditional expression                  | Right          |

Assignments are statements, not expressions. Comparison chaining such as `a < b < c` is rejected unless explicitly separated into Boolean conditions.

Short-circuit Boolean operators preserve left-to-right evaluation.

Operands of other ordinary expression operators are also evaluated left-to-right, except where an explicitly documented builtin defines a different evaluation contract.

### **1.5 Grammar conflicts and semantic rejection**

A conforming implementation MUST provide a machine-readable grammar, a lexer conformance corpus and a versioned operator table.

A parser MAY internally use Pratt parsing, LR, recursive descent or another technique, but those implementation choices cannot change accepted syntax.

Ambiguity must be eliminated by grammar rules before SAGE performs type-directed overload selection.

**Acceptance criterion:** Every syntactically valid Edition-2027 input has one structural interpretation, independent of symbol-table contents.

The grammar above establishes the central syntax; a ratified grammar annex must additionally enumerate all lexical productions and retained Edition-2026 declaration alternatives. That annex is necessary before claiming the language grammar is completely closed.

## **NLR-02 — Capsule constructors, methods, overrides and Families**

### **2.1 Capsule construction**

The canonical constructor declaration is `init`.

```
capsule Counter
    isolate logical

    private var count: int

    public init(initial: int)
        self.count = initial

    public routine value(self) gives int
        give self.count

    public routine increment(var self)
        self.count += 1
```

Construction is invoked through the compiler-defined operation `Counter.new(...)`.

```
let counter = Counter.new(10)
```

A constructor returning `void` is infallible except for its declared terminal effects.

A constructor returning `result<void,E>` creates a fallible construction operation with result type `result<own Counter,E>`.

On failed construction, already initialized owned resources receive ordinary cleanup, and no partially constructed object escapes.

All non-optional fields MUST be initialized before construction succeeds.

### **2.2 Method receivers**

| Receiver   | Meaning                          |
| ---------- | -------------------------------- |
| `self`     | Shared, nonmutating access       |
| `var self` | Exclusive mutable access         |
| `own self` | Consume or transfer the instance |

`self` is an explicit receiver but is not a user-defined field.

Static methods omit `self`.

Method lookup searches the nominal type, inherited eligible members, then valid extensions. Extensions cannot override an instance method or access private state without authorization.

### **2.3 Virtual dispatch and overrides**

Methods are statically dispatched unless declared `virtual` or inherited as virtual.

An override requires the `override` modifier.

```
capsule BaseCounter
    isolate logical

    public virtual routine describe(self) gives text
        give "base"

capsule SpecialCounter inherit BaseCounter
    isolate logical

    public override routine describe(self) gives text
        give "special"
```

Overrides MUST preserve:

- Parameter types and modes, except explicitly permitted safe variance.
- Return-type compatibility.
- Ownership obligations.
- Contract preconditions and postconditions.
- Public visibility.
- ABI requirements.
- Security authority.
- Effects within the parent contract.

A derived implementation cannot strengthen required preconditions, weaken guaranteed postconditions, or introduce stronger unadvertised effects.

`final` prevents further overriding. `abstract` requires a conforming implementation before instantiation.

Overload resolution uses exact matches first, followed by permitted contextual literal fitting and explicit generic constraint resolution. Ties are errors; declaration order is never an overload preference.

### **2.4 Family resolution**

Family membership is nominal and explicit.

```
family CounterFamily
    member BaseCounter
    member SpecialCounter
```

A listed member MUST resolve to a declared compatible type by the end of the module's declaration-resolution phase.

Families can overlap, but ambiguous behavioral requirements require explicit resolution. Membership never establishes subtype inheritance by itself.

Single-state inheritance is permitted. Multiple behavioral inheritance is permitted through validated capabilities and Family contracts.

Parent-private storage remains private.

**Isolation strengthening is permitted; isolation weakening is not.** A `deep` subclass of a `logical` base must place inherited protected state within the deep compartment and preserve the base's external behavior through mediated access.

## **NLR-03 — Stable Capsule identity and representation**

Paracine SHALL distinguish an identity-bearing Capsule from an explicitly value-oriented Capsule.

### **3.1 Identity-bearing instances**

Ordinary Capsule construction creates a stable logical object identity.

Identity remains stable across movement of owning handles and is not identical to a memory address.

A runtime identity comprises:

```
ObjectIdentity
    compartment_identity
    object_number
    generation
```

Identity values are opaque. They are not security credentials.

Generations prevent stale handles from referring to a newly created object that reused an internal slot.

### **3.2 Isolation-specific representation**

| Isolation | Representation                                                          | Identity stability             |
| --------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| `logical` | Managed native object with an owning handle and concrete state layout   | Across legal handle moves      |
| `memory`  | Protected storage allocation plus an authority-checked handle           | Across protection transitions  |
| `deep`    | Opaque remote handle referencing a broker-controlled compartment object | Across IPC and handle movement |

The ABI for each handle form is versioned separately.

The compiler MUST NOT expose remote implementation pointers or silently reinterpret protected handles as local references.

### **3.3 Value Capsules**

An explicitly annotated `@value` Capsule may use inline aggregate representation.

Value Capsules have value semantics, not persistent object identity.

Moving an inline value transfers its contents under ordinary ownership rules.

`@value` is restricted to Capsule definitions whose fields, inheritance, isolation and lifecycle contracts permit inline representation. An identity-bearing or deep-isolated Capsule cannot be converted into an inline value Capsule through optimization.

### **3.4 Observable identity**

An operation such as `identity_of(instance)` returns an opaque comparison key, not a pointer.

Two live handles identifying the same object compare equal by identity.

Two separately constructed objects have distinct identities even if their state is identical.

An optimizer MAY eliminate an allocation only when object identity, resource effects, construction behavior, destruction and every other observable obligation remain equivalent.

**Acceptance criterion:** ICF, specialization, inlining and scalar replacement cannot merge two observably distinct Capsule identities.

## **NLR-04 — Checkpoint, revert, rollback, recover and commit**

This is one of the most consequential language resolutions because it affects control flow, ownership, exception handling and memory effects.

### **4.1 Transaction domain**

A checkpoint creates a lexical, bounded reversible-state region.

The compiler determines the mutation footprint from statically established ownership and effect summaries.

Only modifications with a valid reversible contract can participate.

A checkpoint may track owned scalar values, owned aggregates, approved collections and transactional Capsule state.

Arbitrary I/O, foreign mutation, network transmission, irreversible resource disposal and terminal traps are not automatically reversible.

### **4.2 Operational semantics**

Let \\(\sigma\\) denote program state and \\(J\\) an undo journal.

Checkpoint entry establishes a snapshot boundary:

\\[ \langle checkpoint\ c,\sigma\rangle \rightarrow \langle body,\sigma,J_c=\varnothing\rangle \\]

Before each tracked mutation, the journal records sufficient information to restore the preceding state.

The permitted outcomes are:

| Operation            | Observable behavior                                                            |
| -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Normal completion    | Commit tracked mutations                                                       |
| `commit @c`          | Commit and exit the checkpoint body                                            |
| `revert @c`          | Restore the checkpoint-entry state and continue within the active body         |
| `rollback @c`        | Restore state and exit the checkpoint body                                     |
| `rollback @c fail E` | Restore state and propagate the typed error                                    |
| Recoverable failure  | Restore state, then propagate the error or invoke a matching `recover` handler |
| Terminal trap        | Terminate the process without guaranteed restoration or cleanup                |

A successful `give`, `break` or ordinary scope exit commits active checkpoint regions before completing the control transfer, unless a more specific failure or rollback rule applies.

### **4.3 Correct rollback example**

```
routine update_stock(var stock: int, amount: int)
    gives result<void, StockError>

    checkpoint @stock_change
        stock += amount

        if stock < 0
            rollback @stock_change
                fail StockError.invalid_stock

    give
```

The multiline presentation above illustrates the operation; the canonical grammar uses the single-statement form:

```
rollback @stock_change fail StockError.invalid_stock
```

This distinction matters because code after a plain rollback is not executed inside the abandoned checkpoint body.

### **4.4 Nested checkpoints**

Nested checkpoints use an ordered journal stack.

If checkpoint B is created inside checkpoint A:

- Successful B commits its changes into A's still-reversible transaction history.
- Rolling back B restores only B's entry state.
- Rolling back A restores all changes since A began, including those committed by B.
- B cannot outlive A.
- Only an active, lexically enclosing checkpoint may be named.
- An error escaping B causes B to roll back before A handles or propagates the error.

Journal memory allocation MUST succeed before the corresponding mutation is made observable.

Failure to reserve required journal resources produces a recoverable error before mutation.

### **4.5 Recovery handlers**

```
checkpoint @operation
    perform_reversible_work()
recover error
    handle_failure(error)
```

A matching `recover` handler executes after automatic restoration and ordinary eligible cleanup.

The handler begins outside the failed checkpoint but remains inside any enclosing checkpoint.

Errors produced by a recovery handler propagate normally to enclosing scopes.

### **4.6 Durability and external state**

Memory rollback does not imply crash durability.

Persistent transaction guarantees require a separate durable storage contract with write-ahead logging, synchronization and recovery validation.

Cross-Capsule transactions require an explicit coordinated transaction protocol if atomicity across participants is promised.

**Acceptance criterion:** Reference PCIR, optimized PCIR and native code must produce the same results and eligible state transitions under nested commit, rollback, revert and recovery.

## **NLR-05 — Deep-isolation method calls and transferred data**

`isolate deep` is a process or comparably qualified protected-compartment boundary, not merely a language visibility modifier.

### **5.1&#x20;**`var self`**&#x20;across processes**

A caller does not receive a direct mutable reference to remote state.

Instead, a `var self` call is lowered into a mediated exclusive invocation.

The runtime SHALL:

1. Validate the caller's authority.
2. Validate that the target object is live.
3. Acquire exclusive access to the target instance.
4. Decode and validate the request.
5. Execute the method in the protected compartment.
6. Commit or restore eligible local state according to its failure contract.
7. Encode the response.
8. Release exclusive access.

The local compiler may represent this as a typed invocation operation, but the remote implementation remains protected.

### **5.2 Borrowed values**

Borrowed `text`, `view<T>` and `span<T>` values cannot be sent as raw pointer-and-length pairs across a deep boundary.

They must be converted into a valid transferable representation.

The standard distinguishes:

- **Copied values:** Serialized into bounded messages.
- **Owned transfers:** Ownership moves under an explicit transfer protocol.
- **Shared protected regions:** Explicit OS-backed shared-memory contracts.
- **Remote references:** Opaque validated handles.

Borrow lifetimes do not magically extend across IPC.

A borrowed argument may be copied for a synchronous call, but the remote compartment cannot retain the originating borrow after that request completes.

### **5.3 Foreign handles**

Raw Windows `HANDLE` values, file descriptors and device identifiers are not portable serialized values.

An explicitly transferable OS handle requires validated duplication, rights reduction, ownership transfer or another supported platform operation.

Failure to establish those conditions rejects the transfer.

### **5.4 Serialization**

Protected messages use a canonical, versioned wire representation with:

- Fixed byte ordering.
- Stable schema identities.
- Length-delimited fields.
- Defined integer and numerical encodings.
- Bounds validation.
- Duplicate-field rules.
- Resource quotas.
- Compatible schema evolution.
- Explicit ownership of transferred capabilities.

Serialized bytes cannot be interpreted as arbitrary in-process object layouts.

### **5.5 Invocation ordering**

Exclusive invocations against the same Capsule object are serialized.

Shared invocations may execute concurrently only when the declared synchronization and object invariants permit it.

Transport failure is reported separately from an application method's typed error.

## **NLR-06 — Typed&#x20;**`ask`**&#x20;and&#x20;**`respond`**&#x20;protocols**

### **6.1 Protocol declaration**

A protocol establishes its request, response and application-error types.

```
protocol BalanceQuery
    request
        account_id: ulong

    response
        balance: long

    error AccountError
```

This declaration creates nominal types:

```
BalanceQuery.Request
BalanceQuery.Response
BalanceQuery.Error
```

Each has a stable schema identity derived from its qualified protocol identity and declared version.

### **6.2 Request syntax**

The canonical synchronous request expression is:

```
let answer = ask account
    with BalanceQuery(account_id: 42)
```

Its result type is:

```
result<
    BalanceQuery.Response,
    ProtocolFailure<BalanceQuery.Error>
>
```

`ProtocolFailure<E>` is a tagged distinction between application errors, authorization refusal, incompatible protocol, transport failure, cancellation, resource exhaustion, deadline expiration and unknown remote outcome.

The compiler must reject protocol requests that do not match the target's authorized protocol set.

### **6.3 Response handler**

A Capsule may provide a handler:

```
public respond BalanceQuery(
    self,
    request: BalanceQuery.Request
) gives result<BalanceQuery.Response, AccountError>

    give BalanceQuery.Response(
        balance: self.balance
    )
```

Inside a response handler, `respond value` is an alternative terminal response operation.

It is not an unrestricted substitute for `give` in ordinary routines.

### **6.4 Scheduling and delivery guarantees**

The default protocol contract is:

| Property         | Required behavior                                                                                        |
| ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Ordering         | FIFO admission for requests from one sender to one target, subject to explicitly documented cancellation |
| Concurrency      | At most one mutable handler per target object                                                            |
| Timeout          | Deadline expiration does not prove remote nonexecution                                                   |
| Cancellation     | Best-effort cancellation of work that has not irreversibly completed                                     |
| Retries          | No implicit automatic retry of effectful requests                                                        |
| Delivery         | A request may fail before delivery or complete remotely without a confirmed response                     |
| Authentication   | Target identity and caller authority validated before execution                                          |
| Resource control | Bounded request sizes, queues and outstanding work                                                       |

Exactly-once execution across crashes is not guaranteed without an additional durable deduplication and transaction protocol.

Nested synchronous requests that would deadlock a serialized target must be rejected when statically provable and otherwise detected or bounded by the runtime.

### **6.5 Chain integration**

Chains may contain `ask` operations.

Their inferred effects, possible failures, serialization costs and ordering constraints participate in dependency analysis.

ACO must not fuse, reorder or eliminate protocol operations merely because their response values appear unused.

## **NLR-07 — Concurrency and formal memory model**

### **7.1 Memory locations and conflicting accesses**

Two accesses conflict when they target overlapping memory locations and at least one modifies storage.

A data race exists when conflicting non-atomic accesses occur in different threads without a happens-before ordering.

**Safe Paracine MUST prevent data races within its documented memory-safety model.**

A compiler-proven safe program cannot create an unprotected conflicting mutable alias through ordinary safe operations.

Unsafe code that violates the memory model has unsafe undefined behavior.

### **7.2 Happens-before relation**

The memory model establishes:

- Sequenced-before order within a thread.
- Synchronizes-with relations through synchronization operations.
- Transitive happens-before relationships.
- Per-atomic-object modification order.
- A global sequentially consistent order for applicable atomic operations.

Ordinary code follows the as-if rule only when these relations and observable behavior are preserved.

### **7.3 Atomic operations**

Paracine defines:

```
relaxed
acquire
release
acq_rel
seq_cst
```

`seq_cst` is the default when no weaker ordering is explicitly requested.

Loads permit relaxed, acquire and sequentially consistent ordering.

Stores permit relaxed, release and sequentially consistent ordering.

Read-modify-write operations permit the relevant supported orderings.

Compare-exchange failure ordering cannot include release semantics and cannot be stronger than permitted by the corresponding successful operation.

Weak compare-exchange may fail spuriously.

### **7.4 Safe shared storage**

Sharing mutable state across threads requires a valid synchronization abstraction.

Examples include:

```
atomic<T>
mutex<T>
rwlock<T>
channel<T>
shared_region<T>
```

The actual library type names and supported atomic widths must be enumerated in the standard library contract.

Unprotected `var` aliases cannot coexist across independently executing threads.

### **7.5 Capsule memory semantics**

Logical Capsules follow ordinary safe-language borrow and concurrency rules.

Memory Capsules additionally enforce their memory-protection policy.

Deep Capsules interact primarily through mediated messages and protected handles.

An explicitly shared memory region connecting protected compartments must establish its own synchronization, ownership and authorization contract.

Process isolation alone does not make shared-memory data-race-free.

### **7.6 PCIR obligations**

PCIR atomics explicitly record:

```
MemoryOrder
AtomicWidth
Alignment
AddressSpace
Provenance
SynchronizationScope
```

ACO cannot substitute an ordinary load for an atomic load unless all required memory-model and observability obligations are discharged.

Volatile accesses do not independently establish interthread synchronization.

**Acceptance criterion:** Litmus tests, race-rejection tests, acquire/release publication tests, sequential-consistency tests and protected shared-memory tests must agree with the formal memory model.

# **PART II — NUMERICAL, ATTRIBUTE AND INTEROPERABILITY RESOLUTIONS**

## **NLR-08 — Decimal and posit numerical semantics**

### **8.1 Decimal floating-point formats**

Paracine SHALL define its decimal types against IEEE 754-2019 decimal arithmetic.

| Type         | Storage width | Decimal precision | Maximum normal adjusted exponent |
| ------------ | ------------- | ----------------- | -------------------------------- |
| `decimal32`  | 32 bits       | 7 digits          | +96                              |
| `decimal64`  | 64 bits       | 16 digits         | +384                             |
| `decimal128` | 128 bits      | 34 digits         | +6144                            |

The proposed canonical interchange encoding is densely packed decimal (DPD).

The language guarantees the same numerical behavior whether an implementation uses hardware instructions or software arithmetic.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://dms-discourse-static.s3.dualstack.us-east-1.amazonaws.com\&sz=32)

dms-discourse-static.s3.dualstack.us-east-1.amazonaws.com+1



### **8.2 Rounding**

The default rounding mode is **round to nearest, ties to even**.

The explicitly supported rounding modes are:

```
nearest_even
nearest_away
toward_zero
toward_positive
toward_negative
```

A rounding-mode change applies only within an explicitly declared numerical evaluation context.

The optimizer cannot move a rounding-sensitive calculation across a change of context unless it proves equivalence.

### **8.3 Exceptional values**

Decimal types support positive and negative infinity, positive and negative zero, quiet NaNs, signaling NaNs and subnormal values.

Paracine SHALL define:

- Division of nonzero finite values by zero produces the appropriately signed infinity and reports divide-by-zero status.
- Zero divided by zero produces a quiet NaN and invalid-operation status.
- Infinity minus itself produces a quiet NaN and invalid-operation status.
- Overflow produces the IEEE-defined rounded result and applicable overflow/inexact status.
- Underflow uses gradual underflow, with tininess determined after rounding.
- Invalid signaling-NaN operations produce a quiet NaN and invalid-operation status.
- Signed-zero results follow the selected rounding mode and the specified operation.
- Normal arithmetic producing a NaN uses a canonical quiet-NaN representation; explicit raw-bit operations may preserve encoded payloads without performing arithmetic.

The five IEEE status categories—invalid, divide-by-zero, overflow, underflow and inexact—are separately represented.

### **8.4 Observable exception status**

Ordinary decimal arithmetic produces defined numerical values without terminal traps.

Exception-status observation is an explicit operation, for example:

```
let outcome = decimal.with_status(
    routine() gives decimal64 => amount / divisor
)

let value = outcome.value
let flags = outcome.flags
```

`FpOutcome<T>` contains the value and accumulated status flags for that evaluation region.

This prevents an optimizer from assuming that observable arithmetic flags can be discarded when status collection is enabled.

### **8.5 Posit profile**

The proposed Edition-2027 supported configurations are:

```
posit<8,ES>
posit<16,ES>
posit<32,ES>
posit<64,ES>
```

where `ES` is a compile-time integer from 0 through 4, subject to the format's available bits.

`ES = 2` uses the Posit Standard (2022) encoding and arithmetic contract.

For other values of `ES`, Paracine defines an extended posit encoding with regime scaling:

\\[ \operatorname{useed}=2^{2^{ES}} \\]

and finite nonexceptional magnitudes represented through the regime, exponent and fraction fields.

The extended format MUST have its own explicit encoding, decoding and conversion test vectors. It cannot inherit compliance status solely from the 2022 standard.

### **8.6 Posit exceptional behavior**

Posit arithmetic is fundamentally different from IEEE floating-point arithmetic.

The canonical posit profile provides:

- A single zero representation.
- One Not-a-Real value, NaR.
- No signed zero.
- No positive or negative infinity.
- Correctly rounded basic arithmetic.
- Saturation to finite extrema under the prescribed rounding rules.
- NaR results for operations with non-real results.
- Defined conversions between supported formats.

For the standard-compatible profile, NaR equality and comparisons follow the Posit Standard's ordering rules, rather than IEEE NaN unordered-comparison rules.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://posithub.org\&sz=32)

posithub.org+1



### **8.7 Numerical optimization legality**

ACO MUST distinguish exact mathematical equivalence from machine-numerical equivalence.

For example:

\\[ (a+b)+c \ne a+(b+c) \\]

may hold under floating-point or posit rounding.

An optimization is legal only when it preserves the relevant:

- Result bits or explicitly relaxed result contract.
- Rounding.
- Exceptional values.
- Signed zero.
- NaN/NaR behavior.
- Observable exception status.
- Defined operation order.

Explicit fused operations such as `fma` and posit quire operations may use their specified single-rounding behavior. An optimizer cannot silently introduce equivalent-looking fusion where it changes a mandated intermediate rounding result.

The Posit Standard expressly constrains operation reordering that would affect rounding.&#x20;

